To develop a PHP code that can connect to MongoDB database, fetch data from it, and export the fetched data as a CSV file, we can follow the following steps:

Step 1: Install MongoDB driver for PHP
In order to connect to MongoDB database from PHP, we need to install MongoDB driver for PHP. This can be done by running the following command in the terminal:
```
$ pecl install mongodb
```

Step 2: Connect to MongoDB server and select database
We can connect to MongoDB server and select the database by using the following code:
```
<?php
// Connection parameters
$hostname = "localhost";
$port = "27017";
$username = "";
$password = "";
$dbname = "mydatabase";

// Connection to MongoDB server
$manager = new MongoDB\Driver\Manager("mongodb://$hostname:$port");

// Select database
$query = new MongoDB\Driver\Command(["listDatabases" => 1]);
$databases = $manager->executeCommand("admin", $query);
$found = false;
foreach ($databases as $db) {
    if ($db->name === $dbname) {
        $found = true;
        break;
    }
}
if (!$found) {
    throw new Exception("Database not found: $dbname");
}
$db = $dbname;
?>
```

Step 3: Fetch data from MongoDB collection
We can fetch data from MongoDB collection by using the following code:
```
<?php
// Fetch data from MongoDB collection
$query = new MongoDB\Driver\Query([]);
$cursor = $manager->executeQuery("$db.collection_name", $query);
$data = [];
foreach ($cursor as $document) {
    $data[] = $document;
}
?>
```

Step 4: Export fetched data as CSV file
We can export the fetched data as CSV file by using the following code:
```
<?php
// Set headers to force download
header('Content-Type: text/csv');
header('Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="data.csv"');

// Open file handler
$file = fopen('php://output', 'w');

if (empty($data)) {
    echo "No data found";
} else {
    // Write header line
    $header = array_keys((array) $data[0]);
    fputcsv($file, $header);

    // Write data lines
    foreach ($data as $row) {
        fputcsv($file, (array) $row);
    }
}

// Close file handler
fclose($file);
?>
```

Step 5: Handle errors that may occur during the process
We can handle errors that may occur during the process by using try-catch block as follows:
```
