Here is a program that reads data from a CSV file and generates a report on the total number of entries, average value of a specific field, and the highest and lowest values of a different field. It also includes error handling for missing or incorrectly formatted data.

```
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class CSVReportGenerator {

    private String filename;
    private String delimiter;
    private String[] headers;
    private String[][] data;

    public CSVReportGenerator(String filename, String delimiter) {
        this.filename = filename;
        this.delimiter = delimiter;
    }

    public void readData() throws IOException {
        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(filename));
        String line;
        int rowCount = 0;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            String[] fields = line.split(delimiter);
            if (rowCount == 0) {
                headers = fields;
                data = new String[1][headers.length];
            } else {
                String[][] newData = new String[rowCount+1][headers.length];
                for (int I = 0; I < rowCount; i++) {
                    newData[i] = data[i];
                }
                data = newData;
            }
            if (fields.length != headers.length) {
                throw new IOException("Incorrect number of fields on line "+rowCount+1);
            }
            for (int I = 0; I < headers.length; i++) {
                data[rowCount][i] = fields[i];
            }
            rowCount++;
        }
        reader.close();
    }

    public String generateReport(String countField, String avgField, String maxField, String minField) throws Exception {
        if (data == null) {
            readData();
        }
        int count = data.length-1;
        double sum = 0;
        double maxVal = Double.MIN_VALUE;
        double minVal = Double.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int I = 1; I < data.length; i++) {
            sum += Double.parseDouble(data[i][headersLookup(avgField)]);
            double val = Double.parseDouble(data[i][headersLookup(maxField)]);
            if (val > maxVal) {
                maxVal = val;
            }
            val = Double.parseDouble(data[i][headersLookup(minField)]);
            if (val < minVal) {
                minVal = val;
            }
        }
        double avg = sum / count;
        String report = "CSV Report:\n";
        report += "Total number of entries: "+count+"\n";
        report += "Average "+avgField+": "+String.format("%.2f", avg)+"\n";
        report += "Highest "+maxField+": "+maxVal+"\n";
        report += "Lowest "+minField+": "+minVal+"\n";
        return report;
    }

    private int headersLookup(String fieldName) throws Exception {
        for (int I = 0; I < headers.length; i++) {
            if (headers[i].equals(fieldName)) {
                return i;
            }
        }
        throw new Exception("Field "+fieldName+" not found in headers");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CSVReportGenerator generator = new CSVReportGenerator("data.csv", ",");
        try {
            String report = generator.generateReport("Field1", "Field2", "Field3", "Field4");
            System.out.println(report);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

}
```

To use this program, simply create an instance of the `CSVReportGenerator` class with the filename and delimiter of your CSV file. Then call the `generateReport` method with the names of the fields you want to calculate statistics for. The program will generate a report string that you can print to the console or display in some other way.

The `readData` method reads in the CSV file and stores it in a two-dimensional array called `data`. It also checks for missing or incorrectly formatted data and throws an `IOException` if necessary.

The `generateReport` method calculates the statistics for the specified fields using the data stored in `data`. It also formats the results into a nicely formatted string for display.

The `headersLookup` method is a helper method that looks up the index of a given field name in the `headers` array.

In the main method, we create an instance of the `CSVReportGenerator` and call `generateReport` with some example field names. If there are any errors during the process, they will be caught and printed to the console.

Here is an implementation of a binary search tree in Java, including a delete function:

```java
public class Node {
    int key;
    Node left, right;

    public Node(int item) {
        key = item;
        left = right = null;
    }
}

public class BinarySearchTree {
    Node root;

    public BinarySearchTree() {
        root = null;
    }

    public void insert(int key) {
        root = insertRec(root, key);
    }

    public void delete(int key) {
        root = deleteRec(root, key);
    }

    public Node search(int key) {
        return searchRec(root, key);
    }

    private Node insertRec(Node root, int key) {
        if (root == null) {
            root = new Node(key);
            return root;
        }

        if (key < root.key)
            root.left = insertRec(root.left, key);
        else if (key > root.key)
            root.right = insertRec(root.right, key);

        return root;
    }

    private Node deleteRec(Node root, int key) {
        if (root == null)
            return root;

        if (key < root.key)
            root.left = deleteRec(root.left, key);
        else if (key > root.key)
            root.right = deleteRec(root.right, key);
        else {
            // node with only one child or no child
            if (root.left == null)
                return root.right;
            else if (root.right == null)
                return root.left;

            // node with two children
            root.key = minValue(root.right);

            root.right = deleteRec(root.right, root.key);
        }

        return root;
    }

    private int minValue(Node root) {
        int minv = root.key;
        while (root.left != null) {
            minv = root.left.key;
            root = root.left;
        }
        return minv;
    }

    private Node searchRec(Node root, int key) {
        if (root == null || root.key == key)
            return root;

        if (root.key > key)
            return searchRec(root.left, key);

        return searchRec(root.right, key);
    }

    public void inorder() {
        inorderRec(root);
    }

    private void inorderRec(Node root) {
        if (root != null) {
            inorderRec(root.left);
            System.out.print(root.key + " ");
            inorderRec(root.right);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BinarySearchTree tree = new BinarySearchTree();

        tree.insert(50);
        tree.insert(30);
        tree.insert(20);
        tree.insert(40);
        tree.insert(70);
        tree.insert(60);
        tree.insert(80);

        System.out.println("Inorder traversal of the given tree");
        tree.inorder();

        System.out.println("\nDelete 20");
        tree.delete(20);
        System.out.println("Inorder traversal of the modified tree");
        tree.inorder();

        System.out.println("\nDelete 30");
        tree.delete(30);
        System.out.println("Inorder traversal of the modified tree");
        tree.inorder();

        System.out.println("\nDelete 50");
        tree.delete(50);
        System.out.println("Inorder traversal of the modified tree");
        tree.inorder();
    }
}
```

In this implementation, the `Node` class represents a single node in the tree, which contains a key value and left and right child nodes. The `BinarySearchTree` class represents the entire tree, and contains methods for insertion, deletion, and search of nodes.

The `insert` method adds a new node to the tree in the correct position based on its key value. The `search` method follows the tree down from the root to find a node with the given key value.

The `delete` method removes a node with a given key value from the tree, and updates the tree structure to maintain the binary search tree property. If the node to be deleted has no children or only one child, it can be simply removed from the tree. If it has two children, the node can be replaced with its inorder successor, which is the smallest node in its right subtree.

The `inorder` method performs an inorder traversal of the tree, which visits the nodes in ascending order based on their key values.

This implementation uses recursive methods to traverse and update the tree, and includes a `main` method to demonstrate the use of the tree.

